# Copy to .env for local development:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored; this file is the checked-in reference for what it may contain.

# Which deployment this build represents. It controls exactly one thing: whether the
# site is indexable. It was the only environment-dependent behaviour in the codebase
# until PK-19; the two Supabase values and PUBLIC_GOOGLE_AUTH_ENABLED below are now
# environment-dependent too, so what is special about this one is narrower than it
# used to be — it is the only one that changes what the built HTML SAYS, rather than
# which project the build talks to or which control it renders.
#
#   production  ->  robots.txt allows crawling and advertises the sitemap
#   anything else, or unset  ->  robots.txt disallows everything
#
# The comparison is against the literal string "production" and nothing else, so
# "Production", "PRODUCTION" and " production" all correctly fall through to
# Disallow. See src/pages/robots.txt.ts for why it is built to fail safe.
#
# Leave this unset (or set it to "local") for day-to-day work — a local build
# should never look indexable. To reproduce the production build exactly, use
# `npm run build:production` rather than editing this file, so the override is
# visible in the command you ran.
PUBLIC_SITE_ENV=local

# Where this build's Supabase project lives, and the publishable ("anon") key used to
# talk to it. Both are PUBLIC by design and safe to ship in a browser bundle — the
# `PUBLIC_` prefix is not a lapse, it is the point. Authorization is enforced by
# row-level security in Postgres, not by keeping this key secret, so there is nothing
# to leak by putting it here. Contrast src/lib/auth/index.ts's doc comment for the key
# that actually is dangerous, which this project has decided never to introduce at all.
#
# The hosted project refs are deliberately absent from this public repository — see
# the README's "Database" section. There are two hosted projects, production and
# staging, with separate keys and separate data, and CI selects between them from an
# environment-scoped secret rather than from anything checked in here.
#
# THESE TWO ARE REQUIRED FOR LOCAL WORK, and left commented out here because there is no
# value that could be filled in for you. Every git worktree runs its own Supabase stack on
# its own ports (see scripts/supabase.sh), so the correct values are specific to your
# checkout and would go stale the moment a second worktree existed. Get them with:
#
#     npm run db:start        # then, any time, to reprint them:
#     npm run db:status
#
# and copy API_URL and PUBLISHABLE_KEY into your own .env under the two names below.
#
# Without them, `npm run dev` serves every auth route as a 500 and `npm test` fails at
# tests/auth-flow.test.ts with a message saying exactly this. That is deliberate: the auth
# suite must never report "skipped". CI does not read a .env at all — the
# .github/actions/local-database composite action exports the same two names from the
# stack it starts, so every workflow that runs the suite is pointed at its own throwaway
# database by construction and can never reach a hosted project.
# PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
# PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Whether the "Continue with Google" button is offered on sign-in and sign-up at all,
# and whether a POST claiming it was clicked is honoured rather than refused outright.
# See src/lib/auth-routes.ts (GOOGLE_AUTH_ENABLED) for the code this gates.
#
# MUST STAY OFF — unset, or anything other than the literal string "true" — until BOTH
# of the following exist:
#
#   1. A Google Cloud OAuth 2.0 client (Web application type), with this project's auth
#      callback — <site>/auth/callback — added as an authorised redirect URI.
#   2. The Google provider switched ON in that environment's own Supabase project
#      (Authentication -> Providers), with that OAuth client's ID and secret entered.
#
# Neither exists in any environment yet. Turning this on before both do is not a quiet
# no-op: Supabase's own authorize endpoint answers an unconfigured provider with raw
# JSON — {"code":400,"error_code":"validation_failed","msg":"Unsupported provider:
# provider is not enabled"} — rendered straight to whoever clicked the button, which is
# worse than not offering the button at all. Leave this unset for local development.
# PUBLIC_GOOGLE_AUTH_ENABLED=
